  "subtitle": "A prepaid card whose value is burned away by the payphone, one dark unit at a time",
  "why_scout_cares": "The Landis+Gyr / Sodeco optical phonecard (the Austrian 'Optel' system, first optical card in 1977) is the museum's clearest example of value-as-consumed-physical-medium. Instead of a chip or magnetic stripe that a machine reads in secret, the card is printed with an embossed optical structure that the payphone literally heats and destroys as you talk. A thermal head burns a visible dark mark into a white strip on the top of the card for every unit you use, so your remaining balance is always there to be read with the eye — and once the strip reaches zero the card is empty forever. There is no reader computation hidden inside the payphone and no central database to check: the card IS the accounting, destroyed in public. The museum has barcodes and stored-value chips (iButton, Barcode Battler, Cauzin Softstrip, TI Magic Wand) but nothing where value is carried as burnable matter on a card and consumed by a physical ritual. It is the strange, secure, obsolete ancestor of every stored-value payment card.",
